## 3. Entropy as ignored microstructure

Boltzmann entropy:

\[
S = k_B \ln W
\]

Shannon entropy:

\[
H = -\sum_i p_i \ln p_i
\]

UAF interpretation:

\[
\boxed{
S =
\text{size of the state space that the macro-model chooses not to predict}
}
\]

Entropy is not simply "disorder".

Entropy is the amount of micro-detail sacrificed so that a higher-level model can remain tractable.

This is why entropy grows under coarse-graining:

\[
S_{L1}
=
S_{L0}
+
\Delta S_{\text{coarse-graining}}
\]

The increase is not mysterious.  
It is the cost of compression.

---

## 4. Second law as free-energy descent

Classical form:

\[
dS_{\text{total}} \ge 0
\]

UAF form:

\[
\boxed{
\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}_{\text{total}} \le 0
}
\]

The second law is reinterpreted as:

> A macroscopic system evolves toward representations that reduce total predictive cost.

Entropy increases because tracking microstates is expensive.  
The system lowers free energy by using coarser and more stable predictive descriptions.

