## 7. Phase transitions as model-switching

At a phase transition, macroscopic behavior changes abruptly.

UAF interpretation:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Phase transition}
=
\text{switch to a new predictive attractor}
}
\]

The system changes representation because the previous macro-model no longer minimizes free energy.

Examples:

| Phase transition | UAF interpretation |
|---|---|
| Liquid → gas | local-binding predictor loses stability |
| Ferromagnetism | spin alignment becomes lower-F representation |
| Superconductivity | collective quantum coherence becomes optimal |
| Bose-Einstein condensate | many particles collapse into one predictive mode |

Critical temperature marks the point where:

\[
\mathcal{F}_{\text{model A}}
=
\mathcal{F}_{\text{model B}}
\]

After that point, one representation becomes cheaper than the other.

